    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §2803-z, amd §207, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5606\n\nSPONSOR: SANDERS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to requiring hospi-\ntals distribute literature created by the commissioner to patients at a\nhigh risk of sickle cell disease\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nRequires hospitals distribute literature created by the commissioner to\npatients at a high risk of sickle cell disease\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the public health law by adding a new section 2803-z\nrelating to Sickle Cell Disease information distribution.\n\nSection 2 amends subdivision 1 of section 207 the public health law by\nadding a new paragraph which includes Sickle Cell Disease in the Depart-\nment of Health's health care and wellness education and outreach\nprogram.\n\nSection 3 provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nEducation is an easy and inexpensive way to prevent and treat disease.\nSickle cell disease is common among Americans, yet many are not aware of\nthe disease and how certain groups are more at risk of having the trait\nthat could then result in their children having sickle cell.  The\ngovernment has already made steps towards getting the public educated by\nrequiring that all infants get screened for sickle cell, but everyone\nborn before 2006 does not have that same advantage'. An informational\npacket for patients at high risk of developing sickle cell is necessary\nand would be advantageous to Americans as well as the Medicaid program\nthat covers patients who do have sickle cell.\n\nThis brochure distribution in addition to the creation of an advisory\ncouncil made up of people actually suffering from the disease will give\na voice to underrepresented groups. These groups can deal with a multi-\ntude of hardships, being in chronic pain is not visible and it is possi-\nble that some institutions will not believe patients who are struggling.\nBeing well informed about a disease that you may or do have is not a\nprivilege, it should be a right, and that is what this bill establishes.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nJane M Benson, Bradfor L Therell Jr, \"History and current status of\nnewborn screening for hemoglobinopathies,\" National Library of Medicine,\nApr 2010, https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20207263/\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
